steps.text:
The [open window](g:open-window) hypothesis says that for some hours after heavy exercise the immune system is weaker, so germs get in more easily. It rests on three kinds of finding:

- fewer lymphocytes in the blood after exercise, and weaker function of cells tested in a dish;
- lower [salivary IgA](g:salivary-iga);
- more colds reported after marathons (next lesson).

Immune function is often reported to fall by 15 to 70% after heavy exertion [[c:fall-size]], and the idea is widely accepted [[c:accepted]]. But each pillar can be read another way, and that is what the debate is about.
